  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For a permutation PPP of the integers [1,N][1, N][1,N], let QQQ be the unique inverse permutation of PPP (i.e. QPi=iQ_{P_i} = iQPi​​=i).\n\nDefine f(P)=∑i=1N−1[Pi&lt;Pi+1]+∑i=1N−1[Qi&gt;Qi+1]f(P) = \\sum_{i = 1}^{N - 1}[P_i &lt; P_{i + 1}] + \\sum_{i = 1}^{N - 1}[Q_i &gt; Q_{i + 1}]f(P)=∑i=1N−1​[Pi​&lt;Pi+1​]+∑i=1N−1​[Qi​&gt;Qi+1​], where [X][X][X] is 111 if XXX is true, 000 otherwise.\n\nIn other words, f(P)f(P)f(P) is the sum of number of ascents in PPP and descents in QQQ.\n\nGiven NNN and KKK, your task is to find a permutation PPP of the integers [1,N][1, N][1,N] such that f(P)≥Kf(P) \\ge Kf(P)≥K.\n\nThere are only 222 tests in this problem:\n\n\nN=6,K=6N = 6, K = 6N=6,K=6 (sample test)\nN=105,K=1.99⋅105N = 10^5, K = 1.99 \\cdot 10^5N=105,K=1.99⋅105\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains 222 integers - NNN and KKK.\n\n\nOutput Format\nOutput NNN integers - P1,P2,P3,…,PNP_1, P_2, P_3, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,P3​,…,PN​, (1≤Pi≤N)(1 \\le P_i \\le N)(1≤Pi​≤N), a valid permutation of the integers [1,N][1, N][1,N] such that f(P)≥Kf(P) \\ge Kf(P)≥K.",
    "constraints": [
      "Either N=6,K=6N = 6, K = 6N=6,K=6",
      "Or N=105,K=1.99⋅105N = 10^5, K = 1.99 \\cdot 10^5N=105,K=1.99⋅105"
    ],
